Patch: hotPath
Letter: G
Requires: none
Files: src/impl/formatter.js, src/impl/util.js, src/datetime.js
Summary: six fast paths along the route a numeric token takes, none of them observable

G: the numeric path does avoidable work at six points

Last, and grouped, because this is the tidy-up: one idea applied six times, each
taking work out of a step of the route every numeric token walks without
changing what the step means. None of them is worth a reviewer's time alone —
the smallest is worth nothing measurable on either engine — while together they
are most of what is left on a zone-less pattern once the Intl calls (A, B, D, E)
are dealt with.

Along that route, in order:

  formatter.js  num()        a formatter whose numbering system is latn can pad
                             digits itself; PolyNumberFormatter's Intl branch is
                             only needed by the ones that cannot.
  formatter.js  parseFormat  splitting a format string into tokens is pure, and
                             the same few strings recur, so the split is cached
                             rather than redone per value.
  formatter.js  token loop   the loop rebuilt its own state per token; hoisting
                             what does not vary keeps the switch monomorphic.
  util.js       roundTo      every rounding mode is the identity on an integer
                             once digits is non-negative, and an integer is what
                             every numeric token arrives as.
  util.js       padStart     month, day, hour, minute and second all pad to two
                             digits, so a "00".."99" table covers the common
                             case without building either string.
  datetime.js   tsToObj      integer civil math in place of a wrapper Date and
                             seven getter calls.

Two of the six are engine-specific, and they are the weakest part of this patch:
the token loop pays off on V8 and gives a little back on JSC, and the padStart
table is the reverse. They are in because the other four carry the change, and
splitting by engine would mean filing six patches to land four.

C subsumes two of them by construction — parseFormat and the punctuation half of
the token loop — so a tree that already has C gets less from this than the list
above suggests.

